MOUNT(8)                       P32W Unix                        MOUNT(8)

NAME
       mount, umount - mount and dismount file systems

SYNOPSIS
       /etc/mount [special dir]
       /etc/umount special|dir

DESCRIPTION
       mount with two arguments attaches the filesystem on the block
       device special to the directory dir: from then on dir names
       the root directory of that filesystem, and what dir held is
       hidden until the umount. It then appends a line "special dir"
       to /etc/mtab.

       With no arguments mount lists what is mounted: first the
       line /dev/disk on /, then one "special on dir" line for each
       entry in /etc/mtab.

       umount detaches the filesystem named by its device or by the
       directory it is mounted on, as /etc/mtab records it, and
       removes its line from /etc/mtab. The kernel first writes out
       its blocks and marks it clean. A filesystem cannot be
       unmounted while a file on it is open or a process has its
       current directory there.

       Only root may mount or unmount (mount(2)). The filesystem
       must have been made with mkfs(8). In practice the only
       device that can be mounted is the RAM disk /dev/ram: the
       root device /dev/disk is always mounted on /.

       The kernel refuses (EBUSY) to mount a device twice, to mount
       two filesystems on one directory, or to mount on a
       filesystem's root directory (so not on /). At most three
       filesystems can be mounted besides the root (ENOMEM). A
       filesystem that was not unmounted cleanly is mounted with a
       warning on the console to run fsck(8).

EXAMPLES
       /etc/mkfs /dev/ram 256        make a filesystem on the RAM disk
       /etc/mount /dev/ram /mnt      and mount it
       /etc/mount                    list the mounted filesystems
       /etc/umount /mnt              unmount it again

EXIT STATUS
       0 on success; 1 if the mount or unmount fails, or umount's
       argument is not in /etc/mtab; 2 for a usage error.

DIAGNOSTICS
       mount: /dev/ram on /mnt: no filesystem there (mkfs it first)
       umount: /dev/ram: busy (a file or directory on it is in use)
       umount: /mnt is not mounted (by mount)

FILES
       /etc/mtab      the mounted filesystems
       /dev/ram       the RAM disk (256 blocks, empty after a reboot)

SEE ALSO
       mtab(5), mkfs(8), fsck(8), df(1), fs(5), mount(2)

BUGS
       /etc/mtab is not cleared at boot, so after a reboot (or a
       crash) it may still list a filesystem that is no longer
       mounted; umount cannot remove such a line (the kernel
       refuses the unmount), so edit /etc/mtab by hand.

       umount only unmounts what /etc/mtab lists. mount has no
       options: no -r (read-only), -t or -a, and there is no
       /etc/fstab. Only the first 16 lines of /etc/mtab are read.
